'''trachyglossate''' (trak-i-glos′āt), ''a.'' and ''n.'' '''I.''' ''a.'' Having the tongue rough with radular teeth. as an octopod; of or relating to the ''Trachyglossa.''

'''II.''' ''n.'' Any member of the ''Trachyglossa.''

'''Trachylobium''' (trak-i-lō′bi-um), ''n.'' [NL. (Hayne, 1827), so called with ref. to the rough pods; < Gr. τραχύς, rough, + λοβός, pod: see ''lobe.''] A genus of leguminous plants, of the suborder ''Cæsalpinieæ'' and tribe ''Amherstieæ.'' {{smaller|It is characterized by leaves composed of two coriaceous leaflets, and by flowers with caducous bractlets, each with five petals, all stalked, and somewhat equal, or with the two lower ones minute. There are 2 or 3 species, natives of the tropics in eastern Africa and the Mascarene Islands, with one in Asia, there commonly cultivated. They are trees with white flowers panicled at the ends of the branches. See ''copal'' and ''anime'', 2.}}

'''Trachymedusæ''' (trak″i-mē-dū′sē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < Gr. τραχύς, rough, + NL. ''Medusæ.''] In Haeckel's system of classification, an order of acalephs whose marginal bodies or sense-organs are tentaculicysts, and whose genitals are situated in the course of the radial canals. {{smaller|No hydriform trophosome is known to occur. It is composed of such forms as ''Pelagia'', ''Trachynema'', ''Aglaura'', ''Liriope'', and ''Geryonia'' (or ''Carmarina''), and corresponds to a part of the ''Haplomorpha'' of Carus or of the ''Monopsea'' of Allman.}}
